**Answer: Yes.** If $F:[a,b]\to\mathbb{R}$ is continuous and of bounded variation, then its total variation function $V(x):=T_a^x(F)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$.

---

**Proof.**

We use two standard facts about functions of bounded variation:

**(F1) Additivity.** For any $a\le s<t<u\le b$, $\;T_s^u(F)=T_s^t(F)+T_t^u(F)$.

**(F2) Approximation by partitions.** For every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists a partition $P=\{a=t_0<t_1<\cdots<t_n=b\}$ with $S(P):=\sum_{i=1}^n|F(t_i)-F(t_{i-1})|>T_a^b(F)-\varepsilon$. Refining $P$ (adding partition points) can only increase $S(P)$, so we may assume any prescribed point belongs to $P$.

Since $V$ is increasing, the one-sided limits $V(c^-)$ and $V(c^+)$ exist everywhere with $V(c^-)\le V(c)\le V(c^+)$. It suffices to show:

- **Right-continuity:** $V(c^+)=V(c)$ for every $c\in[a,b)$, i.e. $T_c^x(F)\to 0$ as $x\to c^+$.
- **Left-continuity:** $V(c^-)=V(c)$ for every $c\in(a,b]$, i.e. $T_x^c(F)\to 0$ as $x\to c^-$.

---

**Right-continuity.** Fix $c\in[a,b)$ and $\varepsilon>0$.

By (F2), choose a partition $P=\{a=t_0<\cdots<t_n=b\}$ with $S(P)>T_a^b(F)-\varepsilon/2$, refined so that $c=t_k$ for some $k$.

Restricting to $[c,b]$, the sub-sum gives
$$
\sum_{i=k+1}^{n}|F(t_i)-F(t_{i-1})|>T_c^b(F)-\varepsilon/2, \tag{1}
$$
since $T_a^b(F)=T_a^c(F)+T_c^b(F)$ by (F1) and $S(P)=S(P|_{[a,c]})+S(P|_{[c,b]})\le T_a^c(F)+S(P|_{[c,b]})$.

Pick $\delta>0$ with $\delta<t_{k+1}-c$ (so $(c,c+\delta)\subset(c,t_{k+1})$). For any $x\in(c,c+\delta)$, by (F1),
$$
T_c^x(F)=T_c^b(F)-T_x^b(F). \tag{2}
$$

Using (1) in (2):
$$
T_c^x(F)<\Bigl(\sum_{i=k+1}^{n}|F(t_i)-F(t_{i-1})|+\varepsilon/2\Bigr)-T_x^b(F). \tag{3}
$$

The partition $\{x,t_{k+1},\dots,t_n\}$ of $[x,b]$ gives a lower bound
$$
T_x^b(F)\ge|F(t_{k+1})-F(x)|+\sum_{i=k+2}^{n}|F(t_i)-F(t_{i-1})|. \tag{4}
$$

Substituting (4) into (3):
$$
T_c^x(F)<|F(t_{k+1})-F(c)|-|F(t_{k+1})-F(x)|+\varepsilon/2.
$$

By the reverse triangle inequality, $|F(t_{k+1})-F(c)|-|F(t_{k+1})-F(x)|\le|F(c)-F(x)|$, so
$$
T_c^x(F)<|F(c)-F(x)|+\varepsilon/2. \tag{5}
$$

Since $F$ is continuous at $c$, choose $\delta'>0$ so that $|F(x)-F(c)|<\varepsilon/2$ for $|x-c|<\delta'$. Then for $x\in(c,c+\min(\delta,\delta'))$,
$$
T_c^x(F)<\varepsilon/2+\varepsilon/2=\varepsilon. \tag{6}
$$

Hence $V(x)-V(c)=T_c^x(F)\to 0$ as $x\to c^+$, proving right-continuity. $\checkmark$

---

**Left-continuity.** Fix $c\in(a,b]$ and $\varepsilon>0$.

By (F2), choose a partition $P$ with $S(P)>T_a^b(F)-\varepsilon/2$ and $c=t_k$.

Restricting to $[a,c]$:
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{k}|F(t_i)-F(t_{i-1})|>T_a^c(F)-\varepsilon/2. \tag{7}
$$

For $x\in(t_{k-1},c)$, by (F1),
$$
T_x^c(F)=T_a^c(F)-T_a^x(F). \tag{8}
$$

Using (7):
$$
T_x^c(F)<\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{k}|F(t_i)-F(t_{i-1})|+\varepsilon/2\Bigr)-T_a^x(F). \tag{9}
$$

The partition $\{t_0,\dots,t_{k-1},x\}$ of $[a,x]$ gives
$$
T_a^x(F)\ge\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}|F(t_i)-F(t_{i-1})|+|F(x)-F(t_{k-1})|. \tag{10}
$$

Substituting (10) into (9):
$$
T_x^c(F)<|F(c)-F(t_{k-1})|-|F(x)-F(t_{k-1})|+\varepsilon/2\le|F(c)-F(x)|+\varepsilon/2,
$$
again by the reverse triangle inequality. By continuity of $F$ at $c$, for $x$ sufficiently close to $c$ from the left, $|F(c)-F(x)|<\varepsilon/2$, giving
$$
T_x^c(F)<\varepsilon. \tag{11}
$$

Hence $V(c)-V(x)=T_x^c(F)\to 0$ as $x\to c^-$, proving left-continuity. $\checkmark$

---

**Conclusion.** $V(x)=T_a^x(F)$ is both left- and right-continuous at every interior point, right-continuous at $a$, and left-continuous at $b$. Therefore $V$ is continuous on $[a,b]$.

